<setup>
  <function name="g" symbolic="false" >2^x</function>
  <derivative name="gP">$g</derivative>

  <function name="gp" variable="h">$dg_quotient</function>
</setup>


<p>
    Consider the graph of the function <m>g(x) = $g</m>.  Our goal is to understand why it's reasonable to say that <m>g'(x) = 2^x \ln(2)</m>.
</p>

<ol marker="a.">
  <li>
    <p>
      Use the following interactive graph at left to explore the slopes of the lines tangent to the graph of <m>y = g(x)</m> at <m>x=-2,-1,0,1,2</m>.  Drag the point of tangency around and observe what happens to the slope of the tangent line. Then drag the points on the graph on the right so that the <m>y</m>-value of the point is the slope of the tangent line of the corresponding point on the left.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, at the point <m>(-3,0.125)</m>, the slope of the tangent line is <m>0.0866</m>, so if there had been a point on the right graph located at <m>x=-3</m>, you'd drag it down so that the <m>y-</m>value was <m>0.0866</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you have dragged all the points to the correct heights, the black curve that connects the five points is an approximate graph of <m>g'(x)</m>.
    </p>

<sideBySide>

  <graph ymin="-1" ymax="7" xmin="-4.5" xmax="3.5" showNavigation="false" fixAxes="true">
    $g
    <label anchor="(1.75,6)"><m>g(x)=2^x</m></label>
    <point name="A" stylenumber="2" x="-3" y="2^(-3)" showCoordsWhenDragging="false" labelPosition="upperleft">
          <label><m>($A.x,$A.y)</m></label>
          <constrainTo>$g</constrainTo>
          <attractTo threshold=".1">
            <point>(-2,$$g(-2))</point>
            <point>(-1,$$g(-1))</point>
            <point>(0,$$g(0))</point>
            <point>(1,$$g(1))</point>
            <point>(2,$$g(2))</point>
          </attractTo>
    </point>
    <line name="tangent" through="$A" slope="ln(2)*2^($A.x)" stylenumber="2"/>
    <label anchor="(-2.3,4)">tangent line slope = <m>$tangent.slope</m></label>

  </graph>


  <graph ymin="-1" ymax="6" xmin="-3.5" xmax="3.5" showNavigation="false" fixAxes="true">
    <function variables='x'>2^x</function>
    <!-- <label anchor="(1.8,5)"><m>y=2^x</m></label> -->
    <point name="s_n2" stylenumber="5" labelPosition="upperleft">
        (-2,4)
        <constrainTo><line> x=-2</line></constrainTo>
        <attractToGrid dy="0.3" yoffset="$$gP(-2)"/>
        <label><m>($s_n2.x,$s_n2.y)</m></label>
    </point>
    <point name="s_n1" stylenumber="5" labelPosition="top">
        (-1,4)
        <constrainTo><line> x=-1</line></constrainTo>
        <attractToGrid dy="0.3" yoffset="$$gP(-1)"/>
        <label><m>($s_n1.x,$s_n1.y)</m></label>
    </point>
    <point name="s_z0" stylenumber="5" labelPosition="bottom">
      (0,4)
      <constrainTo><line> x=0</line></constrainTo>
      <attractToGrid dy="0.3" yoffset="$$gP(0)"/>
        <label><m>($s_z0.x,$s_z0.y)</m></label>
    </point>
    <point name="s_p1" stylenumber="5" labelPosition="lowerright">
      (1,4)
      <constrainTo><line> x=1</line></constrainTo>
      <attractToGrid dy="0.3" yoffset="$$gP(1)"/>
        <label><m>($s_p1.x,$s_p1.y)</m></label>
    </point>
    <point name="s_p2" stylenumber="5" labelPosition="lowerright">
      (2,4)
      <constrainTo><line> x=2</line></constrainTo>
      <attractToGrid dy="0.3" yoffset="$$gP(2)"/>
        <label><m>($s_p2.x,$s_p2.y)</m></label>
    </point>
    <curve name="tangcurve" through=" (-4,$s_n2.y-0.1297) $s_n2 $s_n1 $s_z0 $s_p1 $s_p2 (3.6,$s_p2.y+5.635) (4,$s_p2.y+8.32)" stylenumber="5"/>
    <legend position="upperleft">
    <label><m>g(x)=2^x</m></label>
    <label forObject="$tangcurve"><m>y\approx g'(x)</m></label>
  </legend>
  </graph>
</sideBySide>



<answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.01" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>
    <award>
        <when matchPartial>
            $s_n2.y = $$gP($s_n2.x) and
            $s_n1.y = $$gP($s_n1.x) and
            $s_z0.y = $$gP($s_z0.x) and
            $s_p1.y = $$gP($s_p1.x) and
            $s_p2.y = $$gP($s_p2.x)
        </when>
    </award>
</answer>
  </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Next, we will use the limit definition of the derivative to estimate <m>g'(0)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          First, apply the rule for <m>g(x)=2^x</m> to fill in the blank below appropriately.  Once you do so correctly, a table will appear showing the value of the difference quotient for the listed small values of <m>h</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle g'(0)=\lim_{h\to 0}\frac{g(0+h)-g(0)}{h}=\lim_{h\to 0}</m><answer name="dg_quotient">(2^h-2^0)/h</answer>
        </p>
<sideBySide width="40%">
  <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
    <row header>
      <cell><m>h</m></cell>
      <cell><m hide="$dg_quotient.creditAchieved != 1">\frac{2^h-1}{h}</m></cell>
    </row>
   <row>
      <cell><m>-0.1</m></cell>
     <cell><mathInput hide="$dg_quotient.creditAchieved != 1" disabled displayDigits="4">$$gp(-0.1)</mathInput></cell>
   </row>
  <row>
      <cell><m>-0.01</m></cell>
    <cell><mathInput hide="$dg_quotient.creditAchieved != 1" disabled displayDigits="4">$$gp(-0.01)</mathInput></cell>
  </row>
  <row>
      <cell><m>-0.001</m></cell>
     <cell><mathInput hide="$dg_quotient.creditAchieved != 1" disabled displayDigits="4">$$gp(-0.001)</mathInput></cell>
  </row>
  <row>
      <cell><m>-0.0001</m></cell>
     <cell><mathInput hide="$dg_quotient.creditAchieved != 1" disabled displayDigits="4">$$gp(-0.0001)</mathInput></cell>
  </row>
  <row>
      <cell><m>0</m></cell>
     <cell><mathInput hide="$dg_quotient.creditAchieved != 1" disabled></mathInput></cell>
  </row>
  <row>
      <cell><m>0.0001</m></cell>
     <cell><mathInput hide="$dg_quotient.creditAchieved != 1" disabled displayDigits="4">$$gp(0.0001)</mathInput></cell>
  </row>
  <row>
      <cell><m>0.001</m></cell>
    <cell><mathInput hide="$dg_quotient.creditAchieved != 1" disabled displayDigits="4">$$gp(0.001)</mathInput></cell>
  </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>0.01</m></cell>
    <cell><mathInput hide="$dg_quotient.creditAchieved != 1" disabled displayDigits="4">$$gp(0.01)</mathInput></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>0.1</m></cell>
      <cell><mathInput hide="$dg_quotient.creditAchieved != 1" disabled displayDigits="4">$$gp(0.1)</mathInput></cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>
</sideBySide>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>  In part (a), the slope of the tangent line at <m>x = 0</m> was <answer>0.693</answer>.
          </p>
        <p>
          From the values that appear in the table in part (b), the best possible estimate of <m>g'(0)</m> is <answer allowedErrorInNumbers=".00002" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>1/2*($$gp(0.0001)+$$gp(-0.0001))</answer>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Use a calculator to compute a decimal approximation of <m>\ln(2)</m>.  Report at least 5 places after the decimal point in your answer. <m>\ln(2)=</m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers=".00001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>ln(2)</answer>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Putting together all the work completed above, the graph of <m>g'(x)</m> looks like it has <choiceInput name="c1" inline>
            <choice credit="1"> the same general shape</choice>
            <choice>a reflected shape</choice>
            <choice>an unrelated shape</choice> 
          </choiceInput> and is growing 
          <choiceInput name="c2" inline>
            <choice>faster than</choice>
            <choice credit="1">more slowly than</choice>
            <choice>at the same rate as</choice> 
          </choiceInput> the graph of <m>g(x)</m>. Thus, it is plausible that <m>g'(x)</m> is 
          <choiceInput name="c3" inline>
            <choice>a downward vertical shift</choice>
            <choice credit="1">a constant multiple less than 1</choice>
            <choice>a constant more than 1</choice>
            <choice>a horizontal translation</choice> 
          </choiceInput> of <m>g(x)</m>. Since <m>g(0)=1</m> and 
          <choiceInput name="c4" inline>
            <choice credit="1"><m>g'(0)\approx \ln(2)</m></choice>
            <choice><m>g'(1)\approx \ln(2)</m></choice>
            <choice><m>g'(0)\approx 1</m></choice> 
          </choiceInput>, it is reasonable to think that <choiceInput name="c5" inline><choice credit="1"><m>g'(x) = 2^x\ln(2)</m>.</choice><choice><m>g'(x)=2^x</m>.</choice></choiceInput>
          <answer><award><when matchPartial>$c1.selectedIndices=1 and $c2.selectedIndices=2 and $c3.selectedIndices=2 and $c4.selectedIndices=1 and $c5.selectedIndices=1</when></award></answer>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>